  "revision": "2026-11",
  "source": "RAC 0-30 MHz Band Plan, effective 1 June 2023; RAC 60 metres page, 28 July 2022. 6 m from the IARU Region 2 plan, as RAC's own 6 m page dates from 1997. rac.ca/operating/bandplans. 2 m, 70 cm and 23 cm: Radio Amateurs of Canada band plans: 2 m approved by the RAC Board October 2020 (The Canadian Amateur, Nov/Dec 2020), 70 cm approved 23 September 1995, 23 cm approved October 1997; rac.ca. Receiver bands (not amateur): summarised from the ITU Radio Regulations Article 5 table of allocations and general knowledge of national use. Broad, and not specific to a licence.",
  "note": "60 m needs Advanced, or Basic with Honours or Morse. It allows 100 W ERP and 2.8 kHz bandwidth. USB voice in the middle block stops at a dial of 5363.7 kHz. On VHF and UHF: Amateurs are secondary on 70 cm (radiolocation primary, 430-450 MHz available) and on 23 cm (1240-1300 MHz). Repeater frequencies are coordinated locally. RAC lists digital voice (DMR, Fusion, D-STAR) with repeaters on 2 m and digital voice with repeaters on 70 cm, and names no digital-voice calling frequency. RAC does not recommend digital hotspots on 2 m. RAC lists no use for 1296.05-1296.4 MHz apart from the 1296.1 calling frequency. FT4 and Q65 markers are WSJT-X defaults, not RAC plan frequencies. The non-amateur bands are only offered on a receiver, and a guide to what's where, not a statement of who may use it.",
